WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.016
Last time we cut a matrix

2
00:00:05.101 --> 00:00:08.487
into rank one pieces, the biggest first.

3
00:00:08.487 --> 00:00:10.265
Here is our A again

4
00:00:10.265 --> 00:00:13.820
it turns the unit circle into an ellipse.

5
00:00:14.170 --> 00:00:16.318
Its two stretches are four

6
00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:18.052
along the long axis

7
00:00:18.052 --> 00:00:20.530
and one along the short axis.

8
00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:23.994
Now a question with a surprising answer.

9
00:00:23.994 --> 00:00:26.252
Of all matrices of rank one

10
00:00:26.252 --> 00:00:28.354
which is the closest to A?

11
00:00:28.354 --> 00:00:31.157
Does finding it need a long search?

12
00:00:31.157 --> 00:00:32.480
Pause and think.

13
00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:37.670
No search is needed.

14
00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:40.890
Keep the first piece and drop the rest.

15
00:00:40.970 --> 00:00:43.788
Four u one v one transpose squeezes

16
00:00:43.868 --> 00:00:46.605
the whole circle onto one segment

17
00:00:46.605 --> 00:00:49.020
the long axis of the ellipse.

18
00:00:49.370 --> 00:00:51.995
Keep k pieces and you get A k

19
00:00:51.995 --> 00:00:53.744
a matrix of rank k.

20
00:00:53.832 --> 00:00:57.244
The Eckart Young theorem says no matrix

21
00:00:57.331 --> 00:00:59.518
of rank k is closer to A.

22
00:00:59.518 --> 00:01:01.443
We will not prove it

23
00:01:01.443 --> 00:01:04.330
but we can see what it measures.

24
00:01:04.910 --> 00:01:08.004
What is left over is the dropped piece

25
00:01:08.004 --> 00:01:10.623
sigma two u two v two transpose.

26
00:01:10.623 --> 00:01:13.003
It always points along u two.

27
00:01:13.003 --> 00:01:15.066
Take the point one, zero.

28
00:01:15.066 --> 00:01:18.240
A sends it to two, zero on the ellipse

29
00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:21.652
the first piece sends it to one point six

30
00:01:21.652 --> 00:01:24.270
zero point eight on the segment.

31
00:01:24.620 --> 00:01:27.239
The gap between them has length

32
00:01:27.323 --> 00:01:29.182
about zero point nine.

33
00:01:29.182 --> 00:01:31.378
Now take the input v two.

34
00:01:31.378 --> 00:01:34.250
The first piece sends it to zero

35
00:01:34.250 --> 00:01:37.206
and the gap is the whole of u two

36
00:01:37.206 --> 00:01:38.220
length one.

37
00:01:38.570 --> 00:01:40.773
No point has a longer gap

38
00:01:40.773 --> 00:01:43.315
the largest gap is sigma two.

39
00:01:43.315 --> 00:01:45.772
In general the error of A k

40
00:01:45.772 --> 00:01:47.212
in the two norm

41
00:01:47.212 --> 00:01:50.686
is the first singular value you dropped.

42
00:01:50.686 --> 00:01:52.634
In the Frobenius norm

43
00:01:52.634 --> 00:01:55.600
which adds up all squared entries

44
00:01:55.684 --> 00:01:58.057
its square is the sum of the

45
00:01:58.141 --> 00:02:00.090
dropped sigmas squared.

46
00:02:00.670 --> 00:02:03.520
Compare a guess that looks natural.

47
00:02:03.602 --> 00:02:06.208
Keep only the first row of A and

48
00:02:06.289 --> 00:02:08.488
set the second row to zero.

49
00:02:08.488 --> 00:02:10.280
That is rank one too

50
00:02:10.361 --> 00:02:12.560
it flattens the circle onto

51
00:02:12.641 --> 00:02:14.270
the horizontal axis.

52
00:02:14.620 --> 00:02:17.154
Look at the input zero, one.

53
00:02:17.154 --> 00:02:19.506
A sends it to three, two.

54
00:02:19.506 --> 00:02:22.673
The guess sends it to three, zero.

55
00:02:22.673 --> 00:02:25.930
The gap is two units straight down

56
00:02:25.930 --> 00:02:27.740
twice the best gap.

57
00:02:28.090 --> 00:02:30.434
Keeping the first column gives

58
00:02:30.512 --> 00:02:33.872
the same matrix here, also at distance two.

59
00:02:33.872 --> 00:02:35.435
The SVD piece wins

60
00:02:35.435 --> 00:02:38.170
and we found it without searching.

61
00:02:38.750 --> 00:02:40.062
Now a real use.

62
00:02:40.149 --> 00:02:42.423
A grey picture is a matrix

63
00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:44.347
of brightness values.

64
00:02:44.347 --> 00:02:46.445
We drew a small sunset

65
00:02:46.445 --> 00:02:50.380
forty eight rows by sixty four columns: sky

66
00:02:50.380 --> 00:02:52.916
sun, a mountain and the sea.

67
00:02:52.916 --> 00:02:55.190
Its rank is thirty eight.

68
00:02:55.540 --> 00:02:58.031
Here are its first ten singular

69
00:02:58.111 --> 00:02:59.316
values as bars.

70
00:02:59.316 --> 00:03:02.209
The first one towers over the rest

71
00:03:02.209 --> 00:03:04.860
and after that they shrink fast.

72
00:03:05.210 --> 00:03:07.386
Each piece costs one column

73
00:03:07.386 --> 00:03:09.174
one row and one sigma

74
00:03:09.174 --> 00:03:12.438
a hundred and thirteen numbers per piece

75
00:03:12.516 --> 00:03:15.392
instead of three thousand and seventy

76
00:03:15.469 --> 00:03:17.490
two for the whole picture.

77
00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:18.998
Keep one piece

78
00:03:18.998 --> 00:03:22.008
and the error is about thirty percent.

79
00:03:22.085 --> 00:03:24.015
The two norm error is the

80
00:03:24.092 --> 00:03:25.867
height of the next bar.

81
00:03:25.867 --> 00:03:28.646
Two pieces: under nineteen percent.

82
00:03:28.646 --> 00:03:31.193
Ten pieces: about seven percent

83
00:03:31.193 --> 00:03:34.280
from eleven hundred and thirty numbers.

84
00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:38.305
How much of the picture does one piece keep?

85
00:03:38.383 --> 00:03:41.124
A tempting answer divides sigma one

86
00:03:41.202 --> 00:03:43.473
by the sum of all the sigmas.

87
00:03:43.473 --> 00:03:45.900
That gives forty nine percent.

88
00:03:46.250 --> 00:03:48.719
But the size of a picture is the

89
00:03:48.796 --> 00:03:50.879
sum of its squared entries

90
00:03:50.956 --> 00:03:52.730
and that equals the sum

91
00:03:52.807 --> 00:03:54.504
of the squared sigmas.

92
00:03:54.504 --> 00:03:57.821
Square the bars, and the first piece keeps

93
00:03:57.899 --> 00:03:59.364
ninety one percent.

94
00:03:59.364 --> 00:04:01.370
The rest are almost flat.

95
00:04:01.950 --> 00:04:04.277
Not every matrix compresses.

96
00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:06.521
Fill a picture of the same

97
00:04:06.604 --> 00:04:08.515
size with random noise

98
00:04:08.598 --> 00:04:10.842
and put its singular values

99
00:04:10.925 --> 00:04:12.670
next to the sunset's.

100
00:04:13.020 --> 00:04:15.823
The noise bars hardly fall at all.

101
00:04:15.906 --> 00:04:18.461
Ten pieces keep ninety nine and

102
00:04:18.544 --> 00:04:20.935
a half percent of the sunset

103
00:04:20.935 --> 00:04:23.243
but only half of the noise.

104
00:04:23.326 --> 00:04:25.799
Compression works because real

105
00:04:25.881 --> 00:04:27.860
pictures have structure.

106
00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:31.564
So: to get the best matrix of rank k

107
00:04:31.564 --> 00:04:34.180
keep the k biggest SVD pieces.

108
00:04:34.265 --> 00:04:36.882
The error is the first singular

109
00:04:36.966 --> 00:04:38.486
value you dropped

110
00:04:38.486 --> 00:04:42.200
and what you keep is measured with squares.

111
00:04:42.550 --> 00:04:43.033
Next

112
00:04:43.033 --> 00:04:47.091
the same idea applied to a table of data

113
00:04:47.091 --> 00:04:49.990
principal component analysis.
